  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)}+{y}^{2}?$",
  "worked_solution": "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\u2207f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={2}^{x} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & ={2}^{x}\\,i+2y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,1),$ $(0,1),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $\u2207f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(-2,1) & ={2}^{-2}\\,i+2\\cdot (1)\\,j=\\frac{1}{4}\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ \u2207f(0,1) & ={2}^{0}\\,i+2\\cdot 1\\,j=i+2\\,j \\\\ \u2207f(2,-1) & ={2}^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot (-1)\\,j=4\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14109119/assets/59c2db214327f1e078c0ddc5770e91db5e3de021b9e16fbf0ae7a29545a633d1.png)",
